[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Page Visibility API: Detecting When a Page is Visible or Hidden"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Page Visibility API provides a way for web developers to determine the current visibility state of a webpage. This allows you to create more efficient and user-friendly web applications by pausing or modifying behavior when a page is hidden, and resuming it when the page becomes visible again. This is especially useful for tasks like video playback, animations, polling, and resource-intensive computations."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Visibility States"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The API defines two primary states:"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`visible`: The page content is at least partially visible to the user. This doesn't necessarily mean the user is actively interacting with it, but it's in the foreground."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`hidden`: The page content is not visible to the user. This could be because the browser window is minimized, the page is in a background tab, or the screen is locked."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Detecting Visibility Changes"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"The core of the Page Visibility API lies in the `document.visibilityState` property and the `visibilitychange` event. You can use these to monitor changes in the page's visibility."},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here's how to use them:"},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"document.addEventListener('visibilitychange', function() {
  if (document.visibilityState === 'visible') {
    console.log('The page is now visible.');
    // Resume operations
  } else {
    console.log('The page is now hidden.');
    // Pause operations
  }
});"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, we attach an event listener to the `visibilitychange` event. Inside the event handler, we check the value of `document.visibilityState`. If it's `visible`, we know the page is now visible, and we can resume any paused operations. If it's `hidden`, we know the page is hidden, and we can pause operations."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Browser Compatibility"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"While the Page Visibility API is widely supported, it's always good practice to check for browser compatibility. You can do this by checking for the existence of the `document.visibilityState` property or the `document.hidden` property (older implementations)."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"if (typeof document.hidden !== 'undefined') { // Opera 12.10 and Firefox 18 and later support
  // Use the Page Visibility API
} else {
  console.log('Page Visibility API is not supported in this browser.');
}"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Note: Some older browsers used the `document.hidden` property (a boolean) and `document.msHidden` or `document.webkitHidden` properties instead of `document.visibilityState`. You might need to handle these cases for maximum compatibility."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Practical Examples"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here are a few practical examples of how you can use the Page Visibility API:"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Pausing Video Playback:** Pause a video when the page is hidden to conserve bandwidth and CPU resources."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"const video = document.getElementById('myVideo');

document.addEventListener('visibilitychange', function() {
  if (document.visibilityState === 'visible') {
    video.play();
  } else {
    video.pause();
  }
});"},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Stopping Animations:** Stop animations or other visual effects when the page is hidden to reduce CPU usage."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"let animationFrameId;

function animate() {
  // Animation logic here
  animationFrameId = requestAnimationFrame(animate);
}

document.addEventListener('visibilitychange', function() {
  if (document.visibilityState === 'visible') {
    animate();
  } else {
    cancelAnimationFrame(animationFrameId);
  }
});

animate(); // Start the animation initially"},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Reducing Network Requests:** Stop polling a server for updates when the page is hidden to conserve bandwidth and server resources."},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":"let intervalId;

function fetchData() {
  // Fetch data from the server
  console.log('Fetching data...');
}

document.addEventListener('visibilitychange', function() {
  if (document.visibilityState === 'visible') {
    intervalId = setInterval(fetchData, 5000); // Poll every 5 seconds
  } else {
    clearInterval(intervalId);
  }
});

intervalId = setInterval(fetchData, 5000); // Start polling initially"},{"order":24,"type":"subheading","text":"Vendor Prefixes (Legacy Browsers)"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"For older browsers that might not fully support the standard API, you might need to check for vendor prefixes. Here's an example of how to handle that:"},{"order":26,"type":"code","text":"let hidden, visibilityChange;
if (typeof document.hidden !== "undefined") { // Opera 12.10 and Firefox 18 and later support
  hidden = "hidden";
  visibilityChange = "visibilitychange";
} else if (typeof document.msHidden !== "undefined") { // IE 10 and later
  hidden = "msHidden";
  visibilityChange = "msvisibilitychange";
} else if (typeof document.webkitHidden !== "undefined") { // Chrome up to 13, Safari up to 6
  hidden = "webkitHidden";
  visibilityChange = "webkitvisibilitychange";
}

function handleVisibilityChange() {
  if (document[hidden]) {
    console.log("Page is hidden");
  } else {
    console.log("Page is visible");
  }
}

if (typeof document.addEventListener === "undefined" || typeof hidden === "undefined") {
  console.log("This demo requires a browser, such as Google Chrome or Firefox, that supports the Page Visibility API.");
} else {
  document.addEventListener(visibilityChange, handleVisibilityChange, false);
}"},{"order":27,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Page Visibility API is a valuable tool for optimizing web applications and improving the user experience. By detecting when a page is visible or hidden, you can adapt your application's behavior to conserve resources and provide a more seamless experience for users."}]
